<p>Part Two runs from the book's printed page 14 through page 24: two reprinted newspaper clippings sharing a single page, seven pages of "Favorite Photos," a mining-accident story, two shorter stories, a poem, and the closing essay, "Here's Laughing at You!" This site's own part division is noted in the book's introduction on the main page for "Hell Cats And She Ghosts."</p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="mining-town-wont-quit">Mining Town Won't Quit</h2>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
    <img src="/images/site/hell-cats-mining-town-wont-quit.jpg" alt="Reprinted newspaper clipping headed 'There'll Always Be a Creede' in script over the large headline MINING TOWN WON'T QUIT, by Webb Trout, a four-column wire service article about Creede, Colorado's mining history and prospects">
    <figcaption>"'There'll Always Be a Creede': Mining Town Won't Quit," by Webb Trout</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p><strong>By WEBB TROUT</strong></p>

  <p>CREEDE, Colo., Nov. 18.&ndash;(UP) &ndash; Historic old Creede will never join the ranks of Colorado ghost mining towns&ndash; "even if the bottom drops completely out of the price of silver."</p>

  <p>That hard-boiled prediction was made Friday by veteran western mining men who have ridden the roller-coaster of silver fortunes and know its ups and downs.</p>

  <p>The forecast of a future for Creede as solid as towering Bachelor mountain was backed by a confident belief that the old town still may have enough silver and other precious metals left to last more than 100 years.</p>

  <p>Bill Jackson, economy-wise treasurer of Creede and Mineral County who was born here fifty-four years ago, pointed out that the once-booming town has gained an international reputation for silver production and said with conviction: "This will never be a ghost town; there'll always be a Creede."</p>

  <p>Creede is a colorful, timemarked little town nestling in the Rio Grande valley of southwest Colorado&ndash;8,844 feet above sea level&ndash;with lofty mountain peaks rising on every side. It still has 500 year-around residents.</p>

  <p>"Most of the folks in our town love the place," said Jackson. "It's their town, they figure to hang on to their stake in Creede."</p>

  <p>Back in the silver boom years of 1893 and 1895, more than 10,000 silver-hungry souls lived in Creede, and yearly production from mines bored into Bachelor mountain ran around 10 million dollars.</p>

  <p>Then the silver panic struck in 1896. The sound of silver drills on the mountain faded out.</p>

  <p>But the stout-hearted citizens of Creede hung on and the economic outlook has gradually improved.</p>

  <p>The 100 miners who dig for valuable silver, lead and zinc on Bachelor mountain now have run annual production "well over the million-dollar mark" and old-timers say "the country up here hasn't even been scratched."</p>

  <p>Biggest activity on the mountain northwest of Creede now centers on a big vein called the Amethyst Fault. The big tunnel portal is a mile from town. Nine miles below Creede is the Colorado Fuel &amp; Iron corporation fluorspar mine at Wagon Wheel Gap.</p>

  <p>The Emperius Mining company of Denver is the "big wheel" in mining operations at Creede, holding leases on mines of the Amethyst Mining company, Creede Mines, Inc., New York &amp; Chance Mining company and the Volunteer mine.</p>

  <p>The group of claims now being worked includes more than 250 mining lodes and produces ore worth more than one million dollars a year.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 14. A wire service (UP) reprint; no source newspaper or year is given on the page, only the "Nov. 18" dateline. Bill Jackson, treasurer of Creede and Mineral County and quoted here, is John's father, William T. "Billy" Jackson Sr.</em></p>
</section>

