  <h2 id="believe-it-or-not">"Believe It Or Not!" by Ripley</h2>

  <figure class="source-doc-image">
    <img src="/images/site/hell-cats-ripley-believe-it-or-not.jpg" alt="Reprinted 1944 Believe It or Not by Ripley newspaper cartoon panel from the Bellingham Herald, with three separate curiosities: a riddle about the left foot and right foot; a drawing of the town of Creede, Colorado captioned as having no doctors, lawyers, ministers, undertakers, bootleggers or criminals; a caption about Jan Baker of the Phil Spitalny All-Girl Orchestra playing two trumpets at once; and a wingless chicken raised by a doctor in Omaha, Nebraska">
    <figcaption>"Believe It or Not!" by Ripley, The Bellingham Herald, Wed., March 8, 1944, page 8</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The clipping is headed "8&emsp;THE BELLINGHAM HERALD, Wed., March 8, 1944" above the "Believe It or Not! by RIPLEY" masthead, and combines three unrelated curiosities in one panel. The one concerning Creede reads, over a drawing of the town: "THE TOWN of CREEDE Colorado (County Seat of Mineral County) HAS NO DOCTORS-LAWYERS-MINISTERS-UNDERTAKERS-BOOTLEGGERS OR CRIMINALS. THE COUNTY HAS NO INDEBTEDNESS BUT DOES HAVE MONEY IN THE TREASURY." The other two items on the same panel, a riddle about a marching foot and a note on a trumpet-playing orchestra member paired with a wingless chicken raised in Omaha, Nebraska, have nothing to do with Creede and are not transcribed further here.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 14, sharing the page with "Mining Town Won't Quit" above. "Curiodditie s," later in this part, refers back to this clipping: "Creede made Ripley's 'Believe It Or Not' in 1944 as you have noted."</em></p>
</section>

