<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="looking-the-devil-right-in-the-eye">Looking The Devil Right In The Eye</h2>

  <p>In the year 1949 my father realized a lifelong dream by leasing the Phoenix lode mining claim on East Willow Creek from the Moffat Estate Company of Denver. He had known one of the discoverers who had advised him of its potential since it was on the strike of the Solomon/Holy Moses fault. Our operation was very low budget with a lot of begging, borrowing and stealing of supplies.</p>

  <p>The first year was a total failure for J. &amp; S. Mining Company but acquainted us with James Muir, Jr. of California and a nephew by marriage of our own Noel "Snuffy" Oates who had a mine on West Willow Creek. Mr. Muir assured us he could get operating capital if we formed a limited partnership in which he would have an interest. He proved to be an able promoter and from this meeting Outlet Mining Company emerged and we leased equipment from John Hamm who had just closed his Crystal Hill mine, a gold venture near Saguache. With this shot in the arm I retimbered the original discovery shaft as far as possible as my brother Scooter and Gavin Skinner tried to drive around a highly altered area in a tunnel I had driven the previous year. This, too, was unsuccessful and we concentrated our efforts on the shaft which we were able to finish cleaning out and retimbering to the floor (82') by July of 1951.</p>

  <p>Assays were encouraging at this depth and energized us into a sinking operation with Gavin on the hoist and Scooter and I in the shaft. As we neared 150' in depth with an average of 2 1/2 feet advance per day it became routine to sink 20' then timber to within 6' of the floor. This meant that, at times, the ladder was 20' or more above us and we had to use the shaft bucket to raise and lower us. The steel airline was our only signaling device to surface with raps on the pipe in proper code to alert Gavin of our needs.</p>

  <p>This was a disaster in the making, but when you are young and foolhardy risks make the subjects of barroom talk. On one particular day we had drilled out the floor with a jack-hammer and loaded the holes with approximately 30 pounds of Gelex dynamite in 15 holes of 3 feet depth. Gavin had hoisted the drill and hoses to surface and returned the bucket to a position above us waiting for our blasting signal to first lower it where we would crawl in it after igniting the fuses to be hoisted to safety.</p>

  <p>I boosted Scooter to a timber where he could then raise himself high enough to signal. I timed the holes by cutting off lesser lengths, notched the ends of the fuse for easy lighting with a carbide lamp and told him to rap the pipe hard, get the bucket headed down and to stop it just above the floor where I could crawl on after lighting (spitting in miner's slang) 15 separate fuse ends. We had never had a problem before and when Scooter rapped the pipe twice I felt safe enough to start spitting. We were wet, muddy and anxious to get out of the hole and into warm sunshine. The Vee cut came first then, in sequence, to two closely spaced holes in the far end to throw muck outward creating a pocket for the shaft bucket when we began mucking again. The bucket didn't move. Scooter signaled again, harder, and again no movement. I tried climbing with my hands braced against the hanging wall and my feet pushing against the footwall hoping to get high enough for Scooter to grab me. I fell back twice and in panic began slashing at the fuse with my knife. Hot tar was sticking to my fingers and I knew the fuse had burned into the hole and time was fast running out. The smoke was so thick I couldn't see the bottom of the bucket but I could hear Scooter rapping the pipe in a frenzy.</p>

  <p>After Gavin had dropped the bucket to where it now rested he began shoveling the day's hoisting out of the way for the next day's production. With the roar of the compressor and being over the edge of the dump he couldn't hear the signals. Fate intervenes at such times. My wife, Inez, had picked up Mom Jo to make a surprise visit to our workings in an old Chevy truck, Gavin saw them approaching and went up to see who it was. As he stood the shovel against shaft timbers he heard the frantic pings on the pipe and lowered the bucket. Scooter and I were choking in the acrid smoke when he sensed the bucket moving and yelled to climb on it when it hit the floor and he would give "emergency up" signals. We were less than 30 feet from the floor when the first blast lifted us and rocks spanged off the metal bucket. More deafening concussions followed with less and less impact until we were out of the smoke and could see daylight above us.</p>

  <p>It was a terrifying experience for all due to my impatience. Scooter could have climbed the ladder to safety and it took a mountain of nerve for him to stay at his post and keep signaling. He was a veteran of Pacific Ocean beachheads with the U.S. Marine Corps in World War II so his valor came naturally.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 22. A near-fatal 1949&ndash;1951 mining accident at the Phoenix claim on East Willow Creek; John's brother "Scooter" is William "Scooter" Jackson, seen elsewhere on this site, and his wife Inez and mother "Mom Jo" (Josephine Jackson) also appear. Mr. Muir, "Snuffy" Oates, John Hamm, and Gavin Skinner are not otherwise identified beyond what is given here.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="short-and-shorter">Short and Shorter</h2>

  <p>I firmly believe that every youth should spend at least three months in the military to learn discipline and respect and no less than ten years married to a female to learn humility. My wife tells me that girls turn into young ladies with those attributes and more by a system of osmosis denied to boys and therefore it is their duty to train males. Having been in the military and married for fifty years I feel qualified to advise that military duty is a less painful learning period.</p>

  <p>The only way I can keep some semblance of equilibrium in our relationship is to threaten to bring one of the water snakes that roam our yard in the summer into the house and turn it loose.</p>

  <p>In the year 1953 my brother, Joel, made one of his infrequent visits to Creede from his Postal duties in Fort Collins to join me in prospecting and fishing. We combined the two in an excursion to Deep Creek and Snowshoe Mountain where we could catch a few pan-sized brook trout and scout prospect holes on the center ridge of the mountain. My wife, Inez, loves to fish and accompanied us to fish where the canyons forked as Joel and I headed up the trail of the west fork.</p>

  <p>We first visited the prospect holes and found nothing more interesting than buttons of magnetite on altered latite so headed back to the creek to fish on the way back. We each had around six plump trout and decided that was enough for the evening meal as we skirted a beaver pond. We scared a hawk into flight that had been starting to rip open a water snake it had caught and Joel draped the snake over his creel for no good reason as we walked on to the pickup where Inez was curled up on the seat with both windows halfway down sleeping soundly. We were going to dress our fish by the creek some 50 yards from the truck and Joel hung his prize over the window nearest my napping bride. We were making small talk as I slit the fish open and Joel cleaned them of entrails and blood when an unearthly scream resounded from the direction of the pickup then another even louder as Inez exited the truck and began backing up the hill.</p>

  <p>We laughed. We laughed loudly and Joel was rolling on the ground pointing so I wouldn't miss any of her act which was the second mistake he made that day. Inez rode in the back on the way home and didn't speak to either of us that night nor to brother Joel for the remainder of his visit.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 22, following "Looking The Devil Right In The Eye" on the same printed page. John's brother Joel Jackson and wife Inez both appear; the water snake is not harmed in the telling.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="conscience">Conscience</h2>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>They tell me if I don't believe I have no hope to e'er receive<br>
    the gift of absolution</p>

    <p>But in my mind, as I perceive, to weigh the guilt and not deceive<br>
    I, alone, make restitution</p>

    <p>For my sins and fabrications, revelry and altercations<br>
    my mind and soul pollution</p>

    <p>It was mine to pick and choose, it was mine to grasp and lose<br>
    in moral destitution.</p>

    <p>In remorse for my behavior should I cry out for a Saviour<br>
    nay, I beg for dissolution</p>

    <p>To regroup for life's next journey, to make first team in the tourney<br>
    and shine in my ablution</p>

    <p>I enbrace re-incarnation as true spiritual salvation<br>
    I see it not as revolution</p>

    <p>But belief in higher being, omnipotent and all seeing<br>
    rebirth, a natural solution</p>
  </div>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 23, set as a single page on a mottled gold background with no other text. "Enbrace" for "embrace" is printed as shown. No note in the text on the occasion or date of writing.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="heres-laughing-at-you">"Here's Laughing at You!"</h2>

  <figure class="source-doc-image source-doc-image--compact">
    <img src="/images/site/hell-cats-author-and-phil-bethke.jpg" alt="Black and white photograph of two men smiling, a younger man in a work shirt and an older man wearing glasses, head and shoulders">
    <figcaption>"The Author and Phil Bethke in a Younger Year"</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>We live in the richest nation on Earth, the most powerful nation on Earth and we enjoy every convenience available to man yet we are embroiled in controversy from coast to coast. The American Civil Liberties Union is ready to pounce on anyone daring to be "politically incorrect". Claims lawyers outnumber medical practitioners and are raring to make you wealthy through litigation if you get whip lash from your fast rise recliner.</p>

  <p>Our brains are programmed to record the most tragic events in our lives to remind us how fragile life is but they are also programmed to record the most humorous and happy times in our lives to help cope with tragedy. In our quest to make every child a scholar and leader in a society of political correctness, from the playground to the oval office, we have forgotten how to laugh at ourselves. This, in itself, is a tragedy because humor is needed to balance our mood swings.</p>

  <p>I was an average student who became a helluva good laborer in a politically incorrect era. I was associated with every faith and race in childhood, the military and during my productive years. We made fun of each other, we laughed at each other and we fought for each other. It strengthened us &ndash;physically and spiritually&ndash;to find that particular niche where our talents could be best utilized.</p>

  <p>Laugh at yourself, laugh at your neighbor and laugh at the simpletons telling us how wrong we are, we all deserve it and it restores a little sanity in our lives. Let the others be politically correct. Call me anything that comes to mind and laugh at me. I might bloody your nose but I promise you I won't sue.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 24, the book's closing essay. Phil Bethke is not otherwise identified. This is the last page of the book; there is no colophon or closing date.</em></p>
</section>
